WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.360
Klarna is in trouble.
The company that pioneered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.360
the Buy Now Pay Later trend, which&nbsp;
has pushed so many into debt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:13.280
seemed to be on top of the world.
A $17 billion IPO, growing revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:18.080
millions of new active users.
Now it’s collapsing.&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.880
Their stock is down 60%.
Their profits are even worse.&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:28.960
But something even weirder is happening.
Klarna is evolving into something else.&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.400
Something much bigger than&nbsp;
just “buy now, pay later”.&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.680
Yet, investors aren’t impressed.
And now, Klarna is facing a major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:48.560
lawsuit, for a potentially misleading IPO.
Klarna is finally getting what they deserve.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:56.560
Our last video on Klarna ended&nbsp;
on a very important moment:&nbsp;

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.040
The IPO, which they had delayed,&nbsp;
because of how bad things were looking.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.840
On the surface, things were looking pretty good!&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:09.520
They passed 100 million active users.
Plus, the longer customers use Klarna, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:16.480
more often they use it. From 2x a year when they&nbsp;
sign up, to as high as 18x a year after 6 years.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:20.080
[Sebastian Siemiatkowski, CEO, Co-Founder]:&nbsp;
“The momentum is undeniable. We're outpacing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.800
competitors, our merchant network is&nbsp;
scaling rapidly, and our next-gen products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:30.960
are reshaping money management for millions.”
But, Klarna was caught in a pretty ironic spot.&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.120
Klarna is basically the face&nbsp;
of “buy now, pay later”.&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:40.480
A merchant will add Klarna to your&nbsp;
checkout, and pay them 3-6% as a commission.&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.840
They then offer the customer the option to buy now&nbsp;
and pay in multiple installments, pay in 30 days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.960
or financing options, as long as 36 months!
The most common was to pay something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.200
in 4 installments though.
But, there’s one crucial detail.&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.160
Klarna fronts the payment for the merchant, and&nbsp;
takes on the debt of what the customer owes.&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.600
So, they still have to get&nbsp;
that money back from the buyer.&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:09.840
Though, this is how they make money. Failed&nbsp;
payments charge $7, their debt has particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:16.080
high interest, as high as 35.9%! They were&nbsp;
even dragged into court for charging €1.20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.640
just for a reminder email.
Klarna is essentially a debt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:27.120
collection company… just with good branding.
But this business model, creates some problems.&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.080
Even when Klarna reports huge revenue,&nbsp;
huge growth, and 100 million users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.760
they still have the problem of that debt.
In 2025, customer credit losses increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:44.960
by 17%, totalling $136 million. This means more&nbsp;
customers are failing to pay back their debt.&nbsp;

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:49.840
When Klarna pays merchants upfront, they&nbsp;
typically do this with their own debt. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.480
with fewer customers repaying their debt,&nbsp;
Klarna's debt is getting more expensive.&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:00.640
Debt financing expenses climbed to&nbsp;
$130 million in Q1 2025, up 15%.&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.960
That's not Klarna's debt, that's the&nbsp;
cost of their debt, like interest.&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.920
Ironic as it sounds, Klarna has&nbsp;
fallen into their own debt trap.&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:18.720
Buy now, with debt, and pay it back later.
$130 million cost of debt, for just one quarter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.680
which is slowly rising. It makes you&nbsp;
wonder how massive Klarna's actual debt is.&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.560
Buy now, pay later users&nbsp;
are also disproportionately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.040
financially vulnerable compared to other debt.&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:34.800
A study this year found that 41% of customers&nbsp;
had been late on a payment, up from last year.&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:40.320
This is why, despite their EBITDA saying they made&nbsp;
$3 million in profit, on 700 million in revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.360
they actually lost $99 million.
In one quarter too!&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:51.200
Not only is this ethically questionable,&nbsp;
it also causes these problems for Klarna.&nbsp;

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:56.880
But that brings us back to potentially the most&nbsp;
important moment for Klarna: The IPO along with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:02.160
CEO and co-founder: Sebastian Siemiatkowski.
They had delayed their IPO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.120
which they said was due to tariffs.
But, I think he knew the Buy now pay later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:13.600
model wasn’t sustainable. At least, on its own.
With how Klarna’s outlook was, I don’t think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:19.120
an IPO would have been hugely successful.
They needed a story for Wall Street to buy.&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:22.720
That’s why, Sebastian had&nbsp;
even bigger plans for Klarna.&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:26.160
He’s had plans for a huge&nbsp;
pivot, into something else.&nbsp;

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:32.800
So Klarna began moving ahead with their IPO.
And… it was a huge success?

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.680
In September 2025, Klarna went public on the NYSE.
Priced at $40,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:44.320
above the guidance range of $35-$37.
Demand exceeded supply by roughly 25 times.&nbsp;

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:51.040
Shares opened at $52 and closed&nbsp;
first day at $45.82, up 15%.&nbsp;

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:57.440
At closing, Klarna was valued at $17.4&nbsp;
billion, raising $1.37 billion total.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:03.680
To outsiders, this seemed like a total home run.
Like all the worries about Klarna were gone.&nbsp;

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:07.920
But, not exactly.
Look at this graph.&nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.880
Their market cap is down more than 50%.&nbsp;

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:15.280
The price is even lower.
Something weird is happening.&nbsp;

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:20.000
And now, Klarna is facing a major&nbsp;
lawsuit because of this IPO.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:32.560
Klarna have been rebranding into something bigger.
If we look at their IPO, they describe themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:37.040
as “the global digital bank and flexible&nbsp;
payments provider” in the first line.&nbsp;

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.680
The second part, is what Klarna&nbsp;
actually does. Flexible payments.&nbsp;

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:47.840
So what’s with the “Global digital bank”?
Klarna is changing, and not everyone’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:50.640
happy about it.
Let’s go a bit deeper.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:55.520
The same week Klarna filed for an IPO,&nbsp;
it partnered with the company OnePay.&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.480
That’s basically Walmart’s payment company,&nbsp;
and Klarna is now the retailer’s exclusive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:04.320
“Buy now pay later” provider.
Prior to this, Walmart exclusively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.120
used Affirm, who has now been dropped.
[Sebastian Siemiatkowski, CEO, Co-Founder]:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:12.000
“Choosing Klarna as their exclusive installment&nbsp;
loans partner at Walmart in the US is a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.680
vote of confidence as we pursue our goal of&nbsp;
being available everywhere for everything.”&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:23.440
But something about all this is… weird.
First, Klarna issued OnePay warrants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:30.720
acquire 15.3 million Klarna ordinary shares, over&nbsp;
five years, with an average price of $34 each.&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.600
That basically means Klarna essentially&nbsp;
paid Walmart in stock for the partnership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:40.400
with a cheaper share price, which&nbsp;
means they really wanted this.&nbsp;

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:45.280
At least, it was a cheaper share price.
But that’s not all.&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:48.880
Repayment options are 3 to 36 months,&nbsp;
with interest rates reportedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:55.680
ranging from 10% to as high as 36%.
Those loans are long and with interest.&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:03.120
In 2024, Klarna said 99% of transactions on&nbsp;
its network were interest-free. But no longer.&nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:07.360
Klarna has been slowly trying to&nbsp;
pivot into larger, regular banking.

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:12.080
Specifically, through what they call: "Fair&nbsp;
Financing", those longer-term installment loans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:17.040
typically spanning 6 to 24 months.
Basically, more of the normal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:20.720
interest based loans.
And Walmart was just the beginning.&nbsp;

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:25.680
In 2025, they also launched a debit card,&nbsp;
“as it looks to diversify its business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:30.400
beyond short-term credit products”
And when did they do all of this?&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:34.080
Right before their IPO, which they&nbsp;
re-filed in September, and rebranded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:41.200
themselves first as a “global digital bank”.
Klarna is “like” a bank… but they still aren’t?&nbsp;

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:47.040
Klarna does have a full banking&nbsp;
license in the EU, but not in the US.&nbsp;

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:54.400
So, they aren’t, but they can offer FDIC-insured&nbsp;
deposits and withdrawals, aka like a normal bank.&nbsp;

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:58.400
How? They go through a partnership&nbsp;
with WebBank, a small financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.960
institution based in Salt Lake City, Utah.
[Sebastian Siemiatkowski, CEO, Co-Founder]: "We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:05.760
are basically a neobank to a large&nbsp;
degree, but people associate us still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:09.680
strongly with buy now, pay later."
They’re doing this, because getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:15.120
your own US bank charter is so slow.
But this partner bank model gives them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:21.280
90% of the benefits, with none of the hassle.
It’s the “Banking-as-a-Service” route. WebBank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:26.400
holds the actual charter, Klarna rents it.
But why do this?&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.280
After all, the US banking industry&nbsp;
is very competitive, with giants like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.720
JPMorgan Chase &amp; Co and Bank of America.
Well, this might be Klarna’s only choice.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:40.400
First, Buy now pay later&nbsp;
loans have terrible margins.&nbsp;

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:44.160
The loans were short, small,&nbsp;
and usually have no interest.&nbsp;

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:47.520
But interest, in finance, is the real money maker.&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:51.280
Plus, we know the rate of late&nbsp;
payments was rising, which likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:56.320
hurt Klarna’s cost of debt, and profit.
And then there’s the second reason.&nbsp;

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:59.520
I don’t think nearly as many&nbsp;
people would’ve invested in Klarna,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:04.640
if they didn’t have a plan like this.
Pivoting to a “digital bank”? That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:16.240
something you can sell to Wall Street.
So, the question is, did it pay off?

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:22.000
We just passed 900,000 subscribers,&nbsp;
and I wanted to say thank you! Thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:26.000
you for watching our videos; we&nbsp;
put a ton of effort into them.&nbsp;

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:30.080
If you’re not subscribed, maybe&nbsp;
you can help us get to 1 million!

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:34.000
We know the IPO was a huge&nbsp;
success, but after that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:37.920
things turned sour pretty quick.
Even if Klarna isn’t “technically”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:42.240
the real bank here, they still hold the&nbsp;
credit risk for this “fair financing”.&nbsp;

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:45.920
Instead of just short term&nbsp;
loans, it’s long term ones too!&nbsp;

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:51.280
Higher revenue, from interest, and&nbsp;
more customers… but also more risk.

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:55.600
A few weeks after the IPO, Klarna&nbsp;
released their Q3 financials.&nbsp;

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:03.280
Gross Merchandise Volume (GMV), $32.7 billion,&nbsp;
up 43% thanks to rising long-term loans.&nbsp;

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:07.120
Plus 4 million card signups.
Revenue was 900 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:13.200
up from 706 million year over year.
But net income was a loss of 95 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:20.480
down from a profit of 12 million year over year.
And, the more important number: TMD — Transaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:25.520
Margin Dollars. Aka, Klarna's profit&nbsp;
on transaction after the direct costs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:30.080
of funding and servicing the loans.
This is basically the key to whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:34.320
Klarna’s core business is working.
This was 281 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:39.520
down from the same quarter the previous year.
But Klarna was confident, so they projected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:46.640
TMD would grow to $390-400M next quarter.
Then came Q4 2025, and Klarna outperformed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:54.320
its guided projections in revenue, and GMV…
But not TMD, though they did come close.&nbsp;

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:56.320
$372 million.
[Sebastian Siemiatkowski, CEO, Co-Founder]: "This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:01.120
quarter's transaction margin dollar result&nbsp;
did not land where we guided. We take that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:06.640
seriously. The acceleration in lending growth&nbsp;
is the primary driver of that outcome.”&nbsp;

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:12.400
Klarna missed their own projections,&nbsp;
but for a really ironic reason.&nbsp;

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:16.640
Fair Financing grew faster than&nbsp;
Klarna's own forecast — and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:22.080
faster growth is what caused the miss.
Based on the nature of fair financing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:29.040
those long loans, they have a big upfront&nbsp;
cost, but that profit lags quite a ways.&nbsp;

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:35.520
In that quarter, there were 2.5 billion in new&nbsp;
loans, which had $80 million in upfront losses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:41.600
with only $40 million in upfront revenue.
This is what’s hurting Klarna right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:46.560
more of this long-term debt than expected.
The actual income, from interest and such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:52.880
on that 2.5 billion, Klarna won’t see for a&nbsp;
while, which is expected to be 180 million.&nbsp;

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:59.120
That is, if borrowers pay as Klarna expect&nbsp;
them to. Just because you own lots of debt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:03.280
with “potential” earnings,&nbsp;
doesn’t mean it’s guaranteed.

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:08.000
The stock by this point, was down&nbsp;
more than 75% from their IPO.&nbsp;

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:14.480
But, something unexpected just happened.
In Q1 of 2026, Klarna finally reported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:18.560
positive income.
Of… $1 million.&nbsp;

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:24.640
On revenue of $1 billion.
TMD was $389 million, more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:29.040
last quarter, but not by much.
Same goes for GMV too.&nbsp;

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:34.640
Fair Financing and being a “bank” was the&nbsp;
story for the “next big step” for Klarna.&nbsp;

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:40.560
But, is it really translating into more profit?
Well, many don’t think so.&nbsp;

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:44.800
Even after a sliver of profit,&nbsp;
the price is still flat.&nbsp;

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:48.000
Maybe there’ll be more profit growth, but so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:52.800
the growth prospects aren’t great.
And there are other problems too.

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:58.080
On Dec 22, 2025, a shareholder&nbsp;
class action was filed against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:02.160
Klarna “claiming the company misled&nbsp;
investors during its September 2025&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:06.800
IPO by downplaying credit risks in its&nbsp;
buy now, pay later lending business.”

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:10.400
Just weeks after the IPO,&nbsp;
Klarna reported a staggering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:15.120
102% year-over-year increase in its&nbsp;
provision for credit losses, causing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:18.240
shares to plunge well below the $40 IPO price.
[Reed Kathrein, Hagens Berman partner]: "When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:22.720
a company's credit loss provisions double&nbsp;
almost immediately after going public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:28.560
it raises serious questions about whether&nbsp;
the IPO documents were truly transparent”&nbsp;

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:33.840
Klarna in response, said that “We&nbsp;
believe the allegations lack merit”.

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:39.920
This lawsuit is ongoing, but it’s pretty&nbsp;
clear many aren’t happy with Klarna’s choices.&nbsp;

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:45.840
And given how the stock essentially&nbsp;
nosedived after the IPO, it’s understandable.

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:51.440
One of the weirdest parts about Klarna though, is&nbsp;
actually their obsession with AI replacing humans.&nbsp;

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:54.960
But these aren't working as well as many thought.&nbsp;

00:13:54.960 --> 00:14:00.960
AI customer service and AI layoffs are actually&nbsp;
backfiring for a lot of companies, and many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:11.520
including Klarna, are starting to regret it.
